      "text": "\n\n /balances will return everything but user dont need everything \nit also increases response time\n\n \noffset Pagination used to retrieve large datasets in smaller chunks (pages) instead of fetching\nall records in a single API response.\nAPI performance, reduces memory consumption, reduces network payload size\n\n\n/balances?page=0&size=150\n\npage => page number that we need\nsize => Maximum number of records per page\n\n{\n  \"content\": [                                         //Actual records returned   \n    // 150 records        \n  ],\n  \"pageable\": {\n    \"pageNumber\": 0,                         //requested page\n    \"pageSize\": 150                              //Requested maximum records per page\n  },\n  \"totalElements\": 1000,             //Total matching records across all pages\n  \"totalPages\": 7,                               //Total number of pages\n  \"numberOfElements\": 150,      //Records returned in current page\n  \"last\": false,                                      //Whether this is the last page\n  \"first\": true                                     //Whether this is the first page\n}\n\n\n\nSELECT *\nFROM business_unit\nLIMIT pageSize  OFFSET  page*pageSize;\n\nOFFSET =page *pageSize ==> it says no.of to skip before fetching \nLIMIT =pageSize ==> it says max no.of elements to fetch\nex:-\nrequest1 ==>page=0 pageSize=150\n\noffset =0*150 meaning skip 0 elements and fetch 150 elements ==> 1 to 150\n\npage =page+1\n\nrequest2==>page=2 pageSize=150\n\noffset =1*150 meaning skip 150 elements and fetch 150 elements ==> 151 to 300\n\n\n\n\n\nPageable pageable = PageRequest.of(0, 150);\n\nPage<BusinessUnit> result =\n        repository.findAll(pageable);\n\nPage and Slice \nPage: Provides total elements and total pages, usually requiring an additional count query.\nSlice: Tells whether another page exists without necessarily calculating the total number of\nrecords.\n\n\n\n\nCursor pagination: Uses a reference to the last retrieved record to fetch the next set of records.\nIt can perform better for very large datasets and frequently changing data. \n\n\nGET /business-units?lastId=0&size=150\n\n{\n  \"content\": [\n         // actual records \n  ],\n  \"nextCursor\": 150,         // ID of the last returned record. \n  \"hasNext\": true          // does next page has elements\n}\n\n\nSELECT *\nFROM business_unit\nWHERE id > 150\nORDER BY id ASC\nLIMIT 150;\n\n\n\n\nOffset\n\"Skip the first N records and fetch the next N records.\"\nCursor\n\"Continue fetching records after the last record I received.\"\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n\n /balances will return everything but user dont need everything \nit also increases response time\n\n \noffset Pagination used to retrieve large datasets in smaller chunks (pages) instead of fetching all records in a single API response.\nAPI performance, reduces memory consumption, reduces network payload size\n\n\n/balances?page=0&size=150\n\npage => page number that we need\nsize => Maximum number of records per page\n\n{\n  \"content\": [                                         //Actual records returned   \n    // 150 records        \n  ],\n  \"pageable\": {\n    \"pageNumber\": 0,                         //requested page\n    \"pageSize\": 150                              //Requested maximum records per page\n  },\n  \"totalElements\": 1000,             //Total matching records across all pages\n  \"totalPages\": 7,                               //Total number of pages\n  \"numberOfElements\": 150,      //Records returned in current page\n  \"last\": false,                                      //Whether this is the last page\n  \"first\": true                                     //Whether this is the first page\n}\n\n\n\nSELECT *\nFROM business_unit\nLIMIT pageSize  OFFSET  page*pageSize;\n\nOFFSET =page *pageSize ==> it says no.of to skip before fetching \nLIMIT =pageSize ==> it says max no.of elements to fetch\nex:-\nrequest1 ==>page=0 pageSize=150\n\noffset =0*150 meaning skip 0 elements and fetch 150 elements ==> 1 to 150\n\npage =page+1\n\nrequest2==>page=2 pageSize=150\n\noffset =1*150 meaning skip 150 elements and fetch 150 elements ==> 151 to 300\n\n\n\n\n\nPageable pageable = PageRequest.of(0, 150);\n\nPage<BusinessUnit> result =\n        repository.findAll(pageable);\n\nPage and Slice \nPage: Provides total elements and total pages, usually requiring an additional count query.\nSlice: Tells whether another page exists without necessarily calculating the total number of records.\n\n\n\n\nCursor pagination: Uses a reference to the last retrieved record to fetch the next set of records. It can perform better for very large datasets and frequently changing data. \n\n\nGET /business-units?lastId=0&size=150\n\n{\n  \"content\": [\n         // actual records \n  ],\n  \"nextCursor\": 150,         // ID of the last returned record. \n  \"hasNext\": true          // does next page has elements\n}\n\n\nSELECT *\nFROM business_unit\nWHERE id > 150\nORDER BY id ASC\nLIMIT 150;\n\n\n\n\nOffset\n\"Skip the first N records and fetch the next N records.\"\nCursor\n\"Continue fetching records after the last record I received.\"\n\n\n",
